WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:06.520
Speaker 1: There is a new report that former
President Donald Trump is now actively working

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:11.260
to remove Mitch McConnell as the leader of
Republicans in the Senate.

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:14.030
Now, what's funny is Trump's worth.

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:17.960
That work ethic we learned with him as president
is a disaster.

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:22.430
So the fact that Trump believes is believed
to be actively working toward something may

00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:24.370
not really mean very much.

00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:29.130
But regardless, let's talk about what is being
reported at this point in time.

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:36.140
Business Insider reporting Trump is actively
working to oust McConnell as the Senate Republican

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:37.140
leader.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:41.850
Now, remember, because Republicans don't currently
control the Senate, Mitch is the Senate minority

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:44.210
leader rather than the Senate majority leader.

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:49.100
OK, just just a point of clarification to
ensure that we have clarity this evening.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:55.050
Former President Donald Trump has spoken recently
with Republican senators and political allies

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:59.850
about ousting Minority Leader Mitch McConnell
from leadership and is gauging whether there's

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:04.750
any interest among lawmakers for a possible
challenge, according to The Wall Street Journal.

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:08.840
There appears to be little support for such
a drastic move, according to the report.

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:13.759
But Trump's actions could potentially morph
into a larger issue for the party, especially

00:01:13.759 --> 00:01:18.750
as the Kentucky Republican hopes to regain
the Senate majority in the 2022 midterm elections.

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:22.380
And the former president continues to float
a potential 2024 bid.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:25.549
So you have these two factors coming together.

00:01:25.549 --> 00:01:30.509
You have Republicans want the Senate back
in 2002, and there doesn't seem to be much

00:01:30.509 --> 00:01:34.329
of an appetite from the base to remove Mitch
McConnell as the leader.

00:01:34.329 --> 00:01:39.609
On the other hand, Donald Trump is thinking
about a run in 2020 for Mitch McConnell, at

00:01:39.609 --> 00:01:44.409
least over the last nine months, has not been
a particularly big friend to Trump ism.

00:01:44.409 --> 00:01:45.749
And you have this conflict.

00:01:45.749 --> 00:01:49.329
Which way are they more likely to go?

00:01:49.329 --> 00:01:55.140
While Trump and McConnell worked together
to fill scores of federal court vacancies

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:59.829
and with conservative jurists, along with
passing the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act and other

00:01:59.829 --> 00:02:04.869
Republican priorities, Trump's intransigence
in accepting his election loss to now President

00:02:04.869 --> 00:02:10.020
Joe Biden and the January six capital riot
deeply strained the relationship between the

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:11.030
two men.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:15.460
That sort of an understatement in the sense
that at every rally Trump has done recently,

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:20.170
which we've covered just about all of them,
he actively goes after Mitch McConnell and

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:24.820
so-called Renaults and talks about how bad
actually Mitch McConnell has been.

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:25.820
Rarely.

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:26.960
Let me put a different way.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:28.490
I don't want to say rarely.

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:35.660
I don't remember even a single instance of
a current or former Republican president being

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:39.960
this hostile to a Republican Senate leader.

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:40.960
It's not even that it's rare.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:42.610
I just don't remember any other instance.

00:02:42.610 --> 00:02:46.240
I'm glad to be reminded of one if you're aware
of one.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:51.280
The article goes on to say, After Trump's
second Senate impeachment trial for incitement

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:57.470
of insurrection for his role in the riot,
McConnell declined to find the president guilty,

00:02:57.470 --> 00:03:00.120
